Is statistics vague?
The question posed at the outset can now be answered. The mathematics is not vague: given clean data and a verified residual assumption, the machinery delivers exactly what it promises. What is fragile is the bridge from data to facts — \(P(\text{Data}\mid H) \equiv P(\text{Fact}\mid H)\) — and everything in this chapter has concerned the load-bearing elements of that bridge:

a model is a crude, deliberate approximation, and the residual is the price of the approximation, not a nuisance to be ignored;
that residual has an assumed distribution, which must be checked rather than taken on faith — and checking becomes harder as data become scarcer;
degrees of freedom govern how much the data can reveal, and a model with too few residual df (the saturated and over-parameterised cases) reveals little or nothing, however perfect its fit;
the classical tests are this logic specialised to particular statistics, and each carries its own assumptions to be verified.

Statistics appears vague only when these elements are skipped and a p-value is reported as though it were a fact. The rest of this course is, in effect, a survey of what to do when the simplest assumptions do not hold — confounded or dependent observations (Days 2 and 7), responses that are not normal (Day 6), and above all the \(n \ll p\) world in which bioinformatics lives (Days 4 to 11). Each is a variation on the single idea with which the chapter began: build a crude model, and take the leftover seriously.

↪ Day 1 — the statistical thesis: a complex data-generating process approximated by a simple model, with variance partitioned between structure and residual.



DEF Unsupervised learning: methods that look for structure in the predictors alone, without a response variable to guide (“supervise”) the search.


a dimension reduction and ordination algorithm: it maps data with many variables into a low (usually 2) dimensional projection;
a qualitative visualization method and an exploratory method;
a tool for finding variables with potential importance for further study.



DEF Ordination: arranging objects along constructed axes so that similar objects lie close together — a term you will meet often in community ecology.

Based on a distance or dissimilarity measure: hierarchical cluster analysis, K-means cluster analysis — today’s lecture;
Based on correlation: principal component analysis, factor analysis — last lecture;
Hybrids: combinations of the above;
Multivariate confirmatory statistics: MANOVA, SEM — methods that test multivariate hypotheses rather than explore.



↪ Day 4 — PCA works from the covariance matrix; clustering works from the distance matrix. Two summaries of the same cloud, answering different questions.



KEY A clustering algorithm always returns clusters, meaningful or not. The output is a proposal, not a test result.
